Do you have a trimmed down version of this issue?

@benvanik or @AWoloszyn  any guidance here. 

cc @hanhanW as well.

Here is the pass level input : https://gist.github.com/MaheshRavishankar/d235b3b79ec07e89167332cb6fc23efd

```
iree-opt --iree-stream-conversion pass_input.mlir
```

repros what I think is the base issue.

```
%143 = stream.tensor.dispatch  
  @decode_bs4$async_dispatch_20::@decode_bs4$async_dispatch_20_elementwise_D_i1xf16xf16[%138](%138, %141, %6)
  : (index, tensor<?xi1>{%138} in !stream.resource<*>{%139},
  tensor<f16> in !stream.resource<*>{%__hoisted_tensor_f16__size})
  -> tensor<?xf16>{%138} in !stream.resource<*>{%142}
%144 = stream.async.transfer %143 : !stream.resource<*>{%142}
  -> to(#hal.device.affinity<@__device_0>) !stream.resource<*>{%142}
%181 = stream.tensor.clone on(#hal.device.affinity<@__device_0>) %144
  : tensor<?xf16>{%138} in !stream.resource<*>{%142} -> tensor<4x?xf16>{%130} in !stream.resource<*>{%180}    
%183 = stream.tensor.dispatch on(#hal.device.affinity<@__device_0>)
  @decode_bs4$async_dispatch_27::@decode_bs4$async_dispatch_27_attention_4x128xDx128xf16_dispatch_tensor_store[%130](%179, %174, %177, %181, %130)
  : (tensor<4x128x128xf16> in !stream.resource<*>{%178},
  tensor<4x128x?x128xf16>{%130} in !stream.resource<*>{%172},
  tensor<4x128x?x128xf16>{%130} in !stream.resource<*>{%175},
  tensor<4x?xf16>{%130} in !stream.resource<*>{%180}, index) -> tensor<4x128x128xf16> in !stream.resource<*>{%182}

```

@benvanik  just want to make sure I am not missing something, the above snippet is the output of `ConvertToStream`. Looks like the `dispatch_20` isnt getting a device affinity. I can look into it more, but that seems to be the bug right?

the way to read the annotations is:
```
// where this op executes
stream.affinities = [#hal.device.affinity<@__device_1>],
// which affinity produces each operand (here two operands)
stream.affinities.operands = [[#hal.device.affinity<@__device_1>], [#hal.device.affinity<@__device_0>]],
// where each operand is used (here operand 1 is consumed on both device 0 and 1, but above indicates its produced on 0)
stream.affinities.operands.usage = [[#hal.device.affinity<@__device_1>], [#hal.device.affinity<@__device_0>, #hal.device.affinity<@__device_1>]],
// same as above for results (if tied this could differ from this op's execution affinity)
stream.affinities.results = [[#hal.device.affinity<@__device_1>]],
// same as above for results indicating the result is only consumed on 1
stream.affinities.results.usage = [[#hal.device.affinity<@__device_1>]]}
```

if you see `[]` for anything that means analysis was undef/indeterminate/didn't resolve in time

@hanhanW I don't think avoiding hoisting is an option here .

I see two things,
1) if we fix this https://github.com/iree-org/iree/issues/22081#issuecomment-3336185834 then the dispatch will get the right device. That should be good enough for the immediate use case.

2) it will still read globals from other device and we have to manage the globals/think about duplicating globals etc. but that seems like a follow up?


@benvanik am I mistaken in reading this. Doing (1) will at least partially fix the problem?

Self-note for today's progress. I re-implemented the change that updates certain dispatch in topology order first, and backward fixes dispatches that only have global inputs. They are not hoisted because it increases memory footprint. However, I failed when such dispatch has multiple uses.

btw, I think we miss implementing the `AffinityOpInterface` for `flow.tensor.reshape` op. It breaks my affinity op chain if we don't implement the interface. It can be easily fixed by adding one line to https://github.com/iree-org/iree/blob/8e46a4f9191cc52d82a6c6672a0bb11e73dd49eb/compiler/src/iree/compiler/ExternalInterfaces/StreamExternalModels.cpp#L270

Few potential solutions:
1. insert transfer ops right after such dispatch. The caveat is that it will introduce a copy from the device to other devices.
2. clone the whole dispatch chain with proper globals. It is harder, but I think I can make it work. We can have a helper class that creates and caches `[producer, affinity]` in a lazy way; perform the update one-by-one. (2) makes more sense than (1) because other devices don't need to wait for the chosen device and copy the data. They can be computed in parallel on their own devices. I'll give it a shot tomorrow. E.g.,

```
Operation* Helper::query(op, affinity) {
  if (op is global) {
     replicate the global and return the load op.
  } else {
     get all producer operands with `affinity`, and clone the op
     return the op.
  }
}

// In update implementation.
operand.assign(helper.query(producer, affinity));
```

Below is the small repro. The inputs of the `@dispatch0` producer chain are all globals and they don't have assigned affinity. They can't be inferred by producer values, because all the inputs are unknown globals. It is not common to have more than one dispatch op in the chain, though.

```mlir
util.global private @device_a : !hal.device
util.global private @device_b : !hal.device
util.global private @global : tensor<f16>
util.func private @indirect_global_load(%arg0: tensor<f16>, %arg1: tensor<f16>) -> (tensor<f16>, tensor<f16>) {
  %global = util.global.load immutable @global : tensor<f16>
  %0 = flow.dispatch @dispatch0(%global) : (tensor<f16>) -> tensor<f16>
  %1 = flow.tensor.transfer %arg0 : tensor<f16> to #hal.device.affinity<@device_a>
  %2 = flow.dispatch @dispatch1(%0, %1) : (tensor<f16>, tensor<f16>) -> tensor<f16>
  %3 = flow.tensor.transfer %arg1 : tensor<f16> to #hal.device.affinity<@device_b>
  %4 = flow.dispatch @dispatch2(%0, %3) : (tensor<f16>, tensor<f16>) -> tensor<f16>
  util.return %2, %4 : tensor<f16>, tensor<f16>
}
```

My [follow-up fix](https://github.com/iree-org/iree/commit/7c45f85ef61e9c0b5498d03a4b702efeb9f84ee8) is not correct because it does not consider multi use in a dispatch op. This should fix the issue; I'll prepare the patch.

```patch
diff --git a/compiler/src/iree/compiler/Dialect/Stream/Transforms/ReplicateGlobalsPerAffinity.cpp b/compiler/src/iree/compiler/Dialect/Stream/Transforms/ReplicateGlobalsPerAffinity.cpp
index a4fa91725d..38b3089f45 100644
--- a/compiler/src/iree/compiler/Dialect/Stream/Transforms/ReplicateGlobalsPerAffinity.cpp
+++ b/compiler/src/iree/compiler/Dialect/Stream/Transforms/ReplicateGlobalsPerAffinity.cpp
@@ -164,6 +164,11 @@ ValuePerAffinityHelper::getOrCreateGlobalForAffinity(
   // The cache was already populated in the constructor.
   Operation *insertionPoint =
       cachedInsertionPointForGlobal.lookup(globalOp.getOperation());
+  if (!insertionPoint) {
+    LDBG() << "failed to find insertion point for global: "
+           << globalOp.getGlobalName();
+    assert(false && "failed to find insertion point for global");
+  }

   // Create the new global and initializer after the insertion point.
   Location loc = globalOp.getLoc();
@@ -516,6 +521,8 @@ void ReplicateGlobalsPerAffinityPass::runOnOperation() {

     IREE::Stream::AffinityAttr executionAffinityAttr = *affinityAttrs.begin();
     LDBG() << "updating operands with the affinity: " << executionAffinityAttr;
+    SmallVector<std::pair<OpOperand *, Value>>
+        updateList;
     for (auto [operand, maybeGlobalOp] : opToGlobalUseMap[affinityOp]) {
       if (maybeGlobalOp) {
         rewriter.setInsertionPoint(affinityOp);
@@ -525,12 +532,19 @@ void ReplicateGlobalsPerAffinityPass::runOnOperation() {
         auto newLoadOp = IREE::Util::GlobalLoadOp::create(
             rewriter, affinityOp.getLoc(), newGlobalOp);
         newLoadOp.setIsImmutable(true);
-        operand->assign(newLoadOp.getLoadedGlobalValue());
+        updateList.push_back({operand, newLoadOp.getLoadedGlobalValue()});
+        // operand->assign(newLoadOp.getLoadedGlobalValue());
       } else {
-        operand->assign(globalPerAffinityHelper.getOrCreateValueForAffinity(
-            operand, executionAffinityAttr));
+        updateList.push_back(
+            {operand, globalPerAffinityHelper.getOrCreateValueForAffinity(
+                          operand, executionAffinityAttr)});
+        // operand->assign(globalPerAffinityHelper.getOrCreateValueForAffinity(
+        //     operand, executionAffinityAttr));
       }
     }
+    for (auto [opOperand, newValue] : updateList) {
+      opOperand->assign(newValue);
+    }
   }
 }
```

